answer_llm:
An assessor should look at the **records of the reviews**, along with **interviews of responsible personnel**, to verify the review was actually performed and that it covered the required items. The standard specifically says to examine records of reviews and interview responsible personnel for these checks, including that the reviews were performed by the right people and at the required frequency. (req. A3.3.3.b, p. 370; req. 12.4.2.b, p. 306)

To confirm the review covered the required items, the evidence should show:
- confirmation that all BAU activities were performed, including A3.2.2, A3.2.6, and A3.3.1;  
- confirmation that personnel were following security policies and operational procedures, such as daily log reviews, ruleset reviews for network security controls, and configuration standards for new systems;  
- documentation of how the reviews were completed and how all BAU activities were verified;  
- collection of documented evidence required for the annual PCI DSS assessment;  
- review and sign-off of results by personnel assigned responsibility for the PCI DSS compliance program; and  
- retention of records and documentation for at least 12 months covering all BAU activities. (req. A3.3.3, p. 370)

For service providers, the same type of evidence should also show reviews of tasks such as daily log reviews, network security control configuration reviews, applying configuration standards to new systems, responding to security alerts, and change-management processes, performed by personnel other than those responsible for the task. (req. 12.4.2, p. 306)
